      "key": "Steiner calls light seen through darkness red, as in his account of illuminated sunrise clouds viewed from a dark place, and darkness seen through light blue, as in dark space viewed through illuminated air. These arrangements anchor his criticism of Newton's explanation of white light. He then treats the eye as a small whole organism: blood supports life, nerves support consciousness, and colour supposedly acts on both. Red is said to provoke destruction and replenishment through oxygen, whereas blue preserves and conveys inner well-being. The argument therefore concerns a living observer, not colour names alone.",
