      "goal": "Follow the argument from three states of consciousness to qualitative colour and a renewed art of painting.",
      "key": "Steiner begins with waking, dreaming and sleeping, understood through the relation of ego and astral body to physical and etheric bodies. In sleep, he says, the ego and astral body belong to the outer world; its colours and sounds are experienced free of the attachment to heavy objects characteristic of waking perception. This spiritual account introduces qualitative counterparts to weight, measure and number: expansion into cosmic space instead of downward gravity; red measuring yellow through its own expansion and change; and numbers whose members require one another. Such relations lead, in his account, to the beings expressing themselves through qualities. His point is broader than a small patch appearing visually heavy: he proposes another way of knowing reality.",
      "context": "The lecture assigns truth to sleep, beauty to dream and goodness to waking. Sleep supplies affinity with outer things; dream forces become memory and artistic weaving; waking permits a distinction between self and world and the choice to obey or refuse. Beauty arises by forming chaos into cosmos. Steiner then traces an artistic change from the weightless gold of icons and Cimabue through Giotto and Raphael to the Madonna's earthly weight and later naturalism. Renewal cannot simply copy the old icon: it must consciously recover free colour. He acknowledges quantitative science's achievements while arguing that reduction to quantities loses the qualities from which art works. These spiritual and historical interpretations organize the lecture's call to paint out of colour.",
      "activity": "Before painting, make a source map with three rows: sleep/truth, dream/beauty, waking/goodness. Explain the reason for each pairing using captures 127–129. Then explain one qualitative counterpart from 125–126 and summarize the icon–Giotto–Raphael argument from 132–134. Only after that, make or describe an original colour study in which form develops from colour relationships. Add a short commentary showing how this conscious beginning differs from copying an icon or colouring a completed outline.",
      "takeaway": "The call to paint out of colour follows a spiritual account of consciousness, qualitative relations and historical change; reconstruct that argument before applying it.",
      "terms": "Qualitative anti-gravity: the expansive tendency attributed to free spiritual qualities. Chaos: free weaving poised between earthly boundaries and cosmic dispersal. Cosmos: chaos given beautiful form. Microcosm: humanity understood as an expression of the larger cosmos. Icon and Madonna: contrasting artistic stages in Steiner's account, rather than a scale of people's value.",
      "reading": "Lecture 11 · Dornach, 29 July 1923 · GA 228 · captures 123–135. Focus: 125–128, 132–134; editorial notes 48–55, captures 151–152.",
